#!/usr/bin/env bun
/**
 * In-container spawn CLI — Fleet Spawn P3, Task 3 (R-P3-2/R-P3-7).
 *
 * Baked into the studio image at /opt/fleet/studio-fleet (Dockerfile.studio),
 * symlinked to /usr/local/bin/fleet — the exact binary name every role's
 * `allowedTools` already grants claude (`Bash(fleet *)`, byte-identical to
 * the string container/server.ts's OWN task image uses for ITS OWN
 * /usr/local/bin/fleet, a wrapper around container/fleet-cli.ts). These are
 * two DIFFERENT binaries sharing one name on purpose: each is scoped to its
 * own container class (task vs studio) and neither image ever installs the
 * other. This file has ZERO relative imports — self-contained, same
 * convention container/fleet-cli.ts and container/server.ts already keep —
 * so nothing about building the studio image depends on the Worker's `src/`
 * tree.
 *
 * `studio-fleet spawn <role>` calls POST /fleet/spawn on the deployed
 * Worker (R-P3-7's machine surface — deliberately OUTSIDE the
 * Access-protected /studio path, since a container holds a spawn token, not
 * an Access service token). The one auth header below is the ENTIRE
 * credential; there is nothing else in this script, matching
 * src/studio/spawn.ts's SPAWN_TOKEN_HEADER/spawnBody contract exactly (kept
 * as a literal here, not imported — see this file's own note above on why).
 *
 * P4a-2 adds the board verbs against `/fleet/tasks*`, the same machine
 * surface with the same one header: `task ls`, `task show <n>`, and `task
 * report <n>` (the §6 envelope, read from stdin). Deliberately NO create verb
 * — a studio does not open tasks; the Worker is the single writer of the
 * board. There is no route here for one, so this is a fact about the server,
 * not a convention this script keeps.
 *
 * Board issue #41 adds `task state <n> <to>`, and narrows the rule above
 * rather than breaking it. The gap it closes was measured: a task a lead was
 * ACTIVELY working sat at `submitted` until a coordinator moved it by hand,
 * so every monitor watching board state read a healthy working studio as
 * stalled — a false negative in the expensive direction. What the lead now
 * has is a ROUTE, not a credential: it POSTs a destination, and the Worker —
 * still the only thing in this system holding a GitHub token — decides and
 * writes the label. The destination is restricted server-side to `working`,
 * `input_required` and `failed` (src/board/board.ts's LEAD_TASK_STATES).
 * `completed` is refused, because that is the verdict of whoever VERIFIES the
 * work and never of whoever did it.
 *
 * Both env vars below are injected by do.ts's studioEnvVars into the tmux
 * SERVER's captured environment at bring-up (FLEET_WORKER_URL from the new
 * WORKER_PUBLIC_URL wrangler var, FLEET_SPAWN_TOKEN from Fleet Spawn P3 Task
 * 1) — every pane, including this one, inherits both with no extra wiring.
 */

const HELP = `usage: fleet spawn <role>
       fleet task ls | fleet task show <n> | fleet task report <n>
       fleet task state <n> <working|input_required|failed>
       fleet memory ls | fleet memory compact

  fleet spawn <role>    Spawn a peer studio under THIS studio's org-chart
                        edges (fleet/blueprint/org.json, enforced server-side
                        by the Worker — this script only proves WHICH studio
                        is asking, never what it may do). Prints the child's
                        id and state on success, the server's error body
                        otherwise.

  fleet task ls         The tasks assigned to THIS studio. Nobody else's.
  fleet task show <n>   One of them, with its brief and every comment.
  fleet task report <n> Post your §6 envelope as a comment on task <n>.
                        The envelope JSON comes from STDIN:

                          fleet task report 71 <<'EOF'
                          {
                            "intent": "result",
                            "status": "ok",
                            "artifacts": [{"kind": "pr", "pr": "84"}],
                            "evidence": ["bun run test -> 1065 passed"],
                            "open_questions": [],
                            "context_digest": ["chose X over Y because ..."],
                            "notes": "caveman prose for the human"
                          }
                          EOF

                        intent: request|result|error|clarify|escalate
                        status: ok|partial|failed|blocked
                        The Worker stamps msg_id, task_id, schema_version and
                        sender — you cannot set them, and you do not need to.

  fleet task state <n> <to>
                        Move YOUR OWN task to working, input_required or
                        failed. Do it the moment you start: a task left at
                        submitted while you work reads as a stalled studio to
                        every monitor watching the board.

                          fleet task state 41 working

                        You cannot mark your own task completed. That is the
                        verdict of whoever VERIFIES the work, not of whoever
                        did it — report your result and let the verifier
                        close it. You also cannot cancel a task or move
                        anybody else's.

  fleet memory ls       Fleet memory, measured: every index line, plus each
                        file's citation count, age and verdict.
  fleet memory compact  Run P5 §9's compaction pass (sprint close). Proposal
                        JSON on STDIN; empty stdin rebuilds the index only:

                          fleet memory compact <<'EOF'
                          {
                            "merges": [{"title": "CF rollout",
                                        "summary": "keep every number verbatim",
                                        "sources": ["studio/a.md", "studio/b.md"]}],
                            "demote": ["studio/c.md"],
                            "promote": [{"path": "skills/x/SKILL.md",
                                         "title": "X", "summary": "...",
                                         "content": "# X\\n...",
                                         "sources": ["studio/d.md"]}]
                          }
                          EOF

                        It opens a PR. It never deletes a memory file: a
                        demoted file MOVES to fleet/memory/archive/ and stays
                        greppable. The Worker refuses any merge that would drop
                        a number, command, date or path the sources carried.

  fleet --help          Show this message.

The Worker is the single writer of the board. You ask, it writes: every verb
above is one HTTP call to it, and this binary holds no GitHub credential at
all. Never label, close or reopen a board issue by hand — not with gh, not
with git, not with curl.

Requires FLEET_WORKER_URL and FLEET_SPAWN_TOKEN in the environment — both are
set automatically by provisioning; their absence means this container was
never provisioned by Fleet Spawn P3 (exit 2).
`;

// Same header name spawn.ts's SPAWN_TOKEN_HEADER exports — see this file's
// own header for why it is a literal here rather than an import.
const SPAWN_TOKEN_HEADER = "X-Fleet-Spawn-Token";

/** One parsed argv -> what to do. Exported so the source-pin test
 *  (test/container.studio-fleet.test.ts) can regex-anchor on real
 *  identifiers instead of loose prose. */
export type Command =
  | { kind: "help" }
  | { kind: "spawn"; role: string }
  | { kind: "task-ls" }
  | { kind: "task-show"; number: number }
  | { kind: "task-report"; number: number }
  // Board issue #41, half two: a lead moves the state of its own task.
  | { kind: "task-state"; number: number; to: string }
  // P5 §9's memory pass, at sprint close. Release Studio's verb.
  | { kind: "memory-ls" }
  | { kind: "memory-compact" }
  | { kind: "usage-error"; message: string };

/** Pure — no env, no I/O. */
export function parseArgs(argv: string[]): Command {
  if (argv.length === 0 || argv[0] === "--help" || argv[0] === "-h") return { kind: "help" };
  if (argv[0] === "task") return parseTaskArgs(argv.slice(1));
  if (argv[0] === "memory") return parseMemoryArgs(argv.slice(1));
  if (argv[0] !== "spawn") {
    return { kind: "usage-error", message: `unknown command "${argv[0]}" — expected "spawn", "task" or "memory"` };
  }
  const role = argv[1];
  if (!role) return { kind: "usage-error", message: "spawn: missing <role>" };
  return { kind: "spawn", role };
}

/**
 * Board issue #41: the states a lead may move its OWN task to. Kept
 * BYTE-IDENTICAL to src/board/board.ts's own LEAD_TASK_STATES, which is the
 * one that enforces — this copy only turns a typo into a usage error instead
 * of a 403, and lists the options in the message. Same literal-not-imported
 * convention as SPAWN_TOKEN_HEADER above; see this file's own header.
 */
const LEAD_TASK_STATES = ["working", "input_required", "failed"];

/** `task <sub> [<n>] [<to>]`. Split out for the same reason cli/fleet.ts's own
 *  parseTask is: it is the only command with a second word. */
export function parseTaskArgs(argv: string[]): Command {
  const [sub, raw, to] = argv;
  if (sub === "ls") return { kind: "task-ls" };
  if (sub !== "show" && sub !== "report" && sub !== "state") {
    return { kind: "usage-error", message: `unknown task command "${sub ?? ""}" — expected ls, show, report or state` };
  }
  if (raw === undefined || !/^[0-9]+$/.test(raw)) {
    return { kind: "usage-error", message: `task ${sub}: missing or non-numeric <n> (the board issue number)` };
  }
  if (sub === "state") {
    if (to === undefined) {
      return { kind: "usage-error", message: `task state: missing <to> — one of ${LEAD_TASK_STATES.join(", ")}` };
    }
    if (to === "completed") {
      // Refused HERE only so a lead gets a sentence instead of a 403. The
      // enforcement is the Worker's (src/board/board.ts's LEAD_TASK_STATES):
      // the Worker refuses it too, before it reads the issue, so editing
      // this line buys nothing.
      return {
        kind: "usage-error",
        message: "a lead may not mark its own task completed — that is the verdict of whoever verifies the " +
          "work, not of whoever did it. Report your result (fleet task report <n>) and let the verifier " +
          "close it. The Worker refuses it too.",
      };
    }
    if (!LEAD_TASK_STATES.includes(to)) {
      return { kind: "usage-error", message: `task state: "${to}" is not yours to set — one of ${LEAD_TASK_STATES.join(", ")}` };
    }
    return { kind: "task-state", number: Number(raw), to };
  }
  return sub === "show" ? { kind: "task-show", number: Number(raw) } : { kind: "task-report", number: Number(raw) };
}

/** `memory [ls|compact]`. Bare `memory` is the survey, same alias the Mac
 *  CLI keeps (src/studio/cli-args.ts): the read is the safe half. */
export function parseMemoryArgs(argv: string[]): Command {
  const sub = argv[0];
  if (sub === undefined || sub === "ls") return { kind: "memory-ls" };
  if (sub === "compact") return { kind: "memory-compact" };
  return { kind: "usage-error", message: `unknown memory command "${sub}" — expected ls or compact` };
}

/**
 * Builds a /fleet/memory* request. Same one header, same no-repo rule as
 * buildTaskRequest above: the Worker resolves the blueprint repo itself from
 * fleet.json, and a studio does not get to name where memory lives.
 */
export function buildMemoryRequest(
  env: SpawnEnv, path: string, body?: string,
): { url: string; init: RequestInit } {
  return {
    url: new URL(`/fleet/memory${path}`, env.workerUrl).toString(),
    init: body === undefined
      ? { method: "GET", headers: { [SPAWN_TOKEN_HEADER]: env.token } }
      : {
        method: "POST",
        headers: { [SPAWN_TOKEN_HEADER]: env.token, "content-type": "application/json" },
        body,
      },
  };
}

export interface SpawnEnv {
  workerUrl: string;
  token: string;
}

/**
 * Resolves the two required env vars, or names exactly which is/are
 * missing. Pure — env is passed in, never read from `process.env` inside
 * this function — so the exit-2 contract is testable with a plain object,
 * no real process state involved.
 */
export function resolveEnv(env: Record<string, string | undefined>): SpawnEnv | { error: string } {
  const workerUrl = env.FLEET_WORKER_URL;
  const token = env.FLEET_SPAWN_TOKEN;
  const missing = [!workerUrl && "FLEET_WORKER_URL", !token && "FLEET_SPAWN_TOKEN"].filter(Boolean);
  if (missing.length > 0) {
    return {
      error: `studio-fleet: missing env ${missing.join(", ")} — ` +
        "this container was not provisioned by Fleet Spawn P3 (run `fleet --help`)",
    };
  }
  return { workerUrl: workerUrl!, token: token! };
}

/** Builds the exact request /fleet/spawn expects — see spawn.ts's own
 *  SPAWN_TOKEN_HEADER/spawnBody doc comments for the contract this mirrors. */
export function buildSpawnRequest(env: SpawnEnv, role: string): { url: string; init: RequestInit } {
  return {
    url: new URL("/fleet/spawn", env.workerUrl).toString(),
    init: {
      method: "POST",
      headers: { [SPAWN_TOKEN_HEADER]: env.token, "content-type": "application/json" },
      body: JSON.stringify({ role }),
    },
  };
}

/**
 * Builds a /fleet/tasks* request. No `repo` is ever sent: the Worker resolves
 * the board repo from the studio this token belongs to, and a studio that
 * NAMED a repo would be refused anyway (src/board/routes.ts's
 * resolveStudioBoardRepo). Same one header as spawn — that is the whole
 * credential on this surface.
 */
export function buildTaskRequest(
  env: SpawnEnv, path: string, body?: string,
): { url: string; init: RequestInit } {
  return {
    url: new URL(`/fleet/tasks${path}`, env.workerUrl).toString(),
    init: body === undefined
      ? { method: "GET", headers: { [SPAWN_TOKEN_HEADER]: env.token } }
      : {
        method: "POST",
        headers: { [SPAWN_TOKEN_HEADER]: env.token, "content-type": "application/json" },
        body,
      },
  };
}

/** One task row as `fleet task ls` prints it. The url is the actionable half
 *  — a lead pastes it into its own envelope's artifacts. */
interface TaskRow { number: number; state: string | null; title: string; url: string }

/**
 * The board's JSON as lines a lead reads. Deliberately not a table: this is
 * read by an LLM in a terminal pane, and a fixed-width table buys nothing that
 * `#n [state] title` does not.
 */
export function formatTaskRows(rows: TaskRow[]): string {
  if (rows.length === 0) return "(no tasks assigned to this studio)";
  return rows.map((t) => `#${t.number}  [${t.state ?? "DRIFT"}]  ${t.title}\n    ${t.url}`).join("\n");
}

async function main(): Promise<void> {
  const cmd = parseArgs(process.argv.slice(2));

  if (cmd.kind === "help") {
    console.log(HELP);
    return;
  }
  if (cmd.kind === "usage-error") {
    console.error(`studio-fleet: ${cmd.message}`);
    console.error(HELP);
    process.exit(1);
  }

  const spawnEnv = resolveEnv(process.env as Record<string, string | undefined>);
  if ("error" in spawnEnv) {
    console.error(spawnEnv.error);
    process.exit(2);
  }

  const built = await buildRequest(spawnEnv, cmd);
  let res: Response;
  try {
    res = await fetch(built.url, built.init);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(`studio-fleet: request to ${built.url} failed: ${err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err)}`);
    process.exit(1);
  }

  const text = await res.text();
  if (!res.ok) {
    console.error(`studio-fleet: ${built.what} failed (${res.status}): ${text}`);
    process.exit(1);
  }

  try {
    console.log(built.render(JSON.parse(text)));
  } catch {
    // Malformed-but-200 is not this script's problem to diagnose further —
    // print whatever the server sent and let the operator read it directly.
    console.log(text);
  }
}

/** One command -> the request to make and how to print what comes back.
 *  Async only because `task report` reads its envelope off stdin. */
async function buildRequest(
  env: SpawnEnv, cmd: Command,
): Promise<{ what: string; url: string; init: RequestInit; render: (json: unknown) => string }> {
  if (cmd.kind === "spawn") {
    const { url, init } = buildSpawnRequest(env, cmd.role);
    return {
      what: "spawn", url, init,
      render: (json) => {
        const status = json as { id?: string; state?: string };
        return `spawned ${status.id ?? "?"} (${status.state ?? "?"})`;
      },
    };
  }
  if (cmd.kind === "task-ls") {
    const { url, init } = buildTaskRequest(env, "");
    return { what: "task ls", url, init, render: (json) => formatTaskRows(json as TaskRow[]) };
  }
  if (cmd.kind === "task-show") {
    const { url, init } = buildTaskRequest(env, `/${cmd.number}`);
    return { what: "task show", url, init, render: (json) => JSON.stringify(json, null, 2) };
  }
  if (cmd.kind === "task-state") {
    // Board issue #41, half two. Only `to` is sent: no `from` (the Worker
    // reads the task's current state itself, so a lead never has to know it
    // and can never send a stale one) and no `repo` (same rule every other
    // board call here follows — the Worker resolves the board from the
    // studio this token belongs to).
    const { url, init } = buildTaskRequest(env, `/${cmd.number}/state`, JSON.stringify({ to: cmd.to }));
    return {
      what: "task state", url, init,
      render: (json) => {
        // Printed from what the WORKER wrote back, never from `cmd.to` — the
        // same "trust what was actually written, not what was asked for"
        // discipline src/studio/task-state.ts keeps on the operator side.
        const moved = json as { state?: string | null };
        return `task ${cmd.number} is now ${moved.state ?? "?"}`;
      },
    };
  }
  if (cmd.kind === "memory-ls") {
    const { url, init } = buildMemoryRequest(env, "");
    return { what: "memory ls", url, init, render: (json) => JSON.stringify(json, null, 2) };
  }
  if (cmd.kind === "memory-compact") {
    // Same stdin channel as `task report` below, for the same reason: a
    // proposal is a multi-line JSON document, and a heredoc survives quotes
    // where a shell-quoted argv element does not. Empty stdin is LEGAL here
    // and means "rebuild the index only" — the bootstrap run.
    const proposal = await Bun.stdin.text();
    const { url, init } = buildMemoryRequest(env, "/compact", proposal.trim() === "" ? "{}" : proposal);
    return {
      what: "memory compact", url, init,
      render: (json) => {
        const r = json as { pr?: number; url?: string; summary?: Record<string, number> };
        const s = r.summary ?? {};
        return `PR #${r.pr ?? "?"}  ${r.url ?? "(no url)"}\n` +
          `merged ${s.merged ?? 0}, demoted ${s.demoted ?? 0}, promoted ${s.promoted ?? 0}; ` +
          `${s.archived ?? 0} file(s) MOVED to fleet/memory/archive/ (none deleted); ` +
          `index now ${s.indexLines ?? 0} line(s)`;
      },
    };
  }
  // task report. The envelope is read from STDIN rather than an argv flag: a
  // lead composes a multi-line JSON document with quotes in it, and a heredoc
  // survives that where a shell-quoted argument does not.
  const body = await Bun.stdin.text();
  if (body.trim() === "") {
    console.error("studio-fleet: task report reads the envelope JSON from stdin — see `fleet --help`");
    process.exit(1);
  }
  const { url, init } = buildTaskRequest(env, `/${cmd.number}/envelope`, body);
  return {
    what: "task report", url, init,
    render: (json) => {
      const posted = json as { url?: string };
      return `reported on task ${cmd.number}: ${posted.url ?? "(no url in response)"}`;
    },
  };
}

// Review fix round 1: main() had no top-level `.catch()` — a malformed
// FLEET_WORKER_URL (e.g. not a valid URL at all) throws synchronously out of
// buildSpawnRequest's `new URL(...)` deep inside main(), which surfaced as a
// raw, multi-line Bun stack dump (file paths, line numbers, "at ...") instead
// of this file's own `studio-fleet: ...` one-line convention every OTHER
// error path above already follows. Same wrapper shape cli/fleet.ts's own
// top-level guard uses (that file's tail, `main().catch(...)`) — same
// prefix, same `err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err)` unwrap.
// Exit 1: this is an unrecoverable/unexpected failure the same way a
// non-2xx spawn response or a network failure already is above (both exit
// 1) — distinct from the DELIBERATE, anticipated exit 2 for absent env,
// which resolveEnv reaches BEFORE any URL is ever constructed and therefore
// can never itself throw here.
if (import.meta.main) {
  main().catch((err) => {
    console.error(`studio-fleet: ${err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err)}`);
    process.exit(1);
  });
}
